# ONNX inference template: what the CUDA EP options actually do, Windows.
#
# Command: .validation/ort-gpu-1.32.0/bin/cuda-ep-options.exe <model.onnx> <W> <H>
#   - a scratch program: builds the graph on the CPU (reference), then on the
#   CUDA EP once per option set, and prints the provider's own serialisation of
#   its options (GetCUDAProviderOptionsAsString), the device memory held before
#   the session, after creating it and after a run (cuMemGetInfo_v2 through
#   nvcuda.dll), the session-creation time and the largest absolute difference
#   from the CPU session.
# Model: .validation/big_conv.onnx - Conv 3->64 k3, Relu, Conv 64->64 k3,
#   Relu, dynamic H/W, checked with onnx.checker (onnx 1.14.0, seed 1234).
# Runtime: Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime 1.32.0 with the GPU package's provider
#   (CUDA 13), as in onnx-inference-cuda-1.32.log.
#
# Caveat on the memory columns: the first CUDA session in a process also creates
#   the context and initialises cuDNN, which is why the first row reads 1044 MB
#   instead of 10 MB. The rows that matter for the comparison are 2-5.
#
# 1. 1920x1080, all five option sets in one process
reference: CPU session, 132710400 values, 1920x1080

